+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Precision:** Cell counts are integers. The optimal load is an integer ceiling. Small discrepancies in total load distribution are inevitable if block sizes do not divide evenly [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Boundaries:** If the number of processors $P$ is greater than the number of blocks $n$, the source suggests ignoring extra processors or subdividing blocks [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Degenerate Input:** If a single block has a cell count greater than $C_{opt}$, that processor will exceed the optimal load significantly. The algorithm does not specify splitting such blocks, so the load balance will be suboptimal for that specific processor [S1, unofficial].
 
-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-- **Processor Count vs Block Count:** If the number of processors is less than the number of blocks, the algorithm assigns multiple blocks to processors. If the number of processors is greater than the number of blocks, some processors may remain idle or receive no blocks [S1, unofficial].
-- **Rounding Errors:** The use of ceiling for `optimal_cells` means the last processor(s) might receive slightly more than the average, or the sum of assigned cells might exceed `Total Cells` if not careful (though the algorithm assigns existing blocks, so the sum is exactly `Total Cells`). The imbalance is minimized but not eliminated.
-- **Consistent Resolution:** The algorithm assumes each block has a consistent internal resolution. It does not subdivide blocks further; if a single block exceeds `optimal_cells`, it must be assigned entirely to one processor, potentially causing imbalance [S1, unofficial].
+## Subtopics
+
+### Uniform Resolution Decomposition
+Assigns equal spatial portions or equal numbers of blocks regardless of resolution. Inefficient for adaptive data as low-resolution processors finish early [S1, unofficial].
+
+### Shuttle Algorithm
+The specific assignment mechanism used within the Cell Count Algorithm, cycling through processors and assigning sorted blocks until capacity is reached [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Sorting blocks takes $O(n \log n)$ where $n$ is the number of blocks. The assignment loop runs $O(n)$ times. Total time is dominated by sorting.
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(n)$ to store block metadata and assignment lists.
-- **Strengths:** Effectively balances computational load for heterogeneous data. Prevents idle time associated with low-resolution blocks finishing early. Simple to implement.
-- **Limitations:** Requires a pre-pass to calculate cell counts. Does not account for network communication costs or data locality, only computational load. If block sizes vary wildly, the granularity of balancing is limited by the smallest block size.
-- **When to use:** Adaptive-resolution datasets where resolution varies significantly across spatial regions.
-- **When not to use:** Uniform resolution data (where simple spatial decomposition suffices) or when communication overhead dominates computation.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Sorting blocks takes $O(n \log n)$. The assignment loop is $O(n)$ as each block is visited once. Total: $O(n \log n)$ [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(n)$ to store block metadata and assignments [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Balances computational load by ensuring each processor renders approximately the same number of cells. Prevents idle time caused by low-resolution blocks finishing early [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement; effective for datasets with known block resolutions; reduces idle time in adaptive-resolution scenarios [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires pre-calculation of cell counts; assumes cell count is a perfect proxy for rendering time (ignores transfer function complexity, etc.); does not handle dynamic load changes during rendering [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** Adaptive-resolution volume datasets where block resolutions vary significantly and static load balancing is required [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When not to use:** Uniform resolution data (where simple spatial decomposition suffices) or dynamic datasets where block costs change unpredictably [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The algorithm was described in the context of volume visualization of time-varying data using parallel, multiresolution, and adaptive-resolution techniques [S1, unofficial]. It addresses the specific problem of load imbalance in adaptive-resolution data sets, where spatial division leads to inefficiency because processors handling low-resolution portions complete work before those handling high-resolution portions [S1, unofficial]. The approach is also referred to as the "shuttle algorithm" for block assignment in the source literature [S1, unofficial].
+The algorithm was described in the context of volume visualization of time-varying data using parallel, multiresolution, and adaptive-resolution techniques [S1, unofficial]. It addresses the specific challenge of distributing adaptive-resolution data among processors to prevent idle time, contrasting with uniform resolution decomposition methods [S1, unofficial].
 
